#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115029 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c6b30e7-fba6-597c-8306-27d707bfc244" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting One Exponent"}] :question/id #uuid "baae925b-d23d-56db-b15f-b438f68df608" :question/math-academy-id "e-4101" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $3^{3x-3}=9^x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $9$ as ${\\color{red}3}^2,$ and then rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3x-3} & ={9}^{x} \\\\ {3}^{3x-3} & ={({3}^{2})}^{x} \\\\ {3}^{3x-3} & ={3}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3}),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-3 & =2x \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115048 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8eebff2-97ca-599b-a819-04ff34f0c1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting Two Exponents"}] :question/id #uuid "e73675af-973f-583e-9909-0eb9630fdcc8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4161" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $9^{3x}=27^{x+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $9={\\color{red}3}^2$ and $27 = {\\color{red}3}^3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{red}3}^2\\right)^{3x}=\\left({\\color{red}3}^3\\right)^{x+2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2(3x)}={3}^{3(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3}),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3x) & =3(x+2) \\\\ 6x & =3x+6 \\\\ 3x & =6 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8eebff2-97ca-599b-a819-04ff34f0c1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting Two Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083bbb43-125e-530a-a0bf-4e386e0fef8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410210} {:answer/id #uuid "2ebed13b-65b6-5f09-b6ac-5843bb401594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410238} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7cd739-dad9-5004-a078-dfdfdfb9ad48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410289} {:answer/id #uuid "c45e66f0-50f2-598c-b5d0-5402c3cbc44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410335} {:answer/id #uuid "e52fbbd7-076f-5ca8-a202-557210987158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c45e66f0-50f2-598c-b5d0-5402c3cbc44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410335} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c131e70-66d3-5684-931b-600e5433ed9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6488fce-f74c-5334-a4ac-d8d71ef00e69" :question/math-academy-id "q-3090" :question/problem "Solve for $n$ where ${16}^{2n}={8}^{n+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $16={2}^{4}$ and $8={2}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({2}^{4})}^{2n}={({2}^{3})}^{n+1}\\,\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{8n} & ={2}^{3(n+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(2)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8n & =3(n+1) \\\\ 8n & =3n+3 \\\\ 5n & =3 \\\\ n & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c6b30e7-fba6-597c-8306-27d707bfc244" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting One Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4655e0e1-97d5-5387-82f5-2a0060583577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333640} {:answer/id #uuid "57bb8d9b-280e-513c-acdb-1014d45f33b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333791} {:answer/id #uuid "8840f7da-ffe1-5988-a8d4-352d29fbe330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334194} {:answer/id #uuid "b242132e-1e99-5659-9c4b-f06d8ee0a1f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334553} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ce3b19-3ccd-51e4-9d8f-e8979ffcdfa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7ce3b19-3ccd-51e4-9d8f-e8979ffcdfa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335160} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdd81fd-15d7-5d1a-9dc4-6f1226cd81c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e3e80cb-8e1b-5722-8654-94f1e02c6077" :question/math-academy-id "q-66872" :question/problem "Solve for $m$ where ${5}^{m-10}={125}^{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $125$ as ${5}^{3},$ and then rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{m-10} & ={125}^{m} \\\\ {5}^{m-10} & ={({5}^{3})}^{m} \\\\ {5}^{m-10} & ={5}^{3m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(5)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m-10 & =3m \\\\ -2m & =10 \\\\ m & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
